{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792946244,"nonce":"ft0PYP1g1u5mT5Li7dFEltP65lA=","message":"Decision (darmie): every new browser session gets its own new branch checked out for the example project, so edits are isolated.\n\nHow it fits:\n- The isolation itself comes from the session: one workspace per visitor (a Durable Object each), seeded from the example's base ref. Nobody's edits reach another visitor or the example repository, branch or not. The branch gives the session a named, diffable change set on top of that base.\n- Name: `playground/\u003csession-id\u003e`, created from the example's pinned ref (a commit, so the example cannot move under a session).\n- A branch needs a repository, so the archive fast path (no history) is followed by `git init` and one commit of the unpacked files as the baseline, then the branch; the diff, revert and history views then work against that baseline. With a full or shallow clone the branch starts from the real commit instead.\n- The branch is local to the session and is never pushed to the example repository. Saving the work is a separate, explicit action for a signed-in person: a fork or a new repository under their GitHub account (needs the write consent described in the issue), pushed from the session; the Rayzor playground can offer it as \"Save to GitHub\".\n- A session that is idle or abandoned is discarded with its branch; limits from 080ea85 apply (repository size, sessions per identity).\nDepends on: 18e2769 (libgit2 in wasm: init, commit and branch already work under Node), 52138fc, 080ea85.","files":null}]}